There are several natural language processing libraries available in Swift that can be used to analyze Shakespeare's plays and sonnets. One popular library is Natural Language Toolkit (NLTK), which provides tools for tokenizing, stemming, and tagging text.
Here's an example code snippet that uses NLTK to analyze the text of Shakespeare's plays and sonnets:
```
import NaturalLanguage
// Load the text of Shakespeare's plays and sonnets
let text = try! String(contentsOfFile: "shakespeare.txt", encoding: .utf8)
// Tokenize the text into individual words
let tokenizer = NLTokenizer(unit: .word)
tokenizer.string = text
let tokens = tokenizer.tokens(for: text.startIndex..<text.endIndex)
// Stem the words to their base form
let stemmer = NLSTEMmer()
let stems = tokens.map { token -> String in
    let stemRange = stemmer.stemRange(for: token, in: text)
    return String(text[stemRange])
}
// Tag the words with their part of speech
let tagger = NLTagger(tagSchemes: [.lexicalClass])
tagger.string = text
let tags = tokens.map { token -> NLTag? in
    let tagRange = tagger.rangeOfTag(.lexicalClass, at: token.lowerBound, unit: .word, scheme: .lexicalClass)
    return tagger.tag(at: tagRange, unit: .word, scheme: .lexicalClass)?.rawValue
}
// Analyze the text for recurring themes and motifs
// You can use the stems and tags to identify patterns in the text
// For example, you could count the frequency of certain stems or tags to identify recurring themes
```
In this example, the code loads the text of Shakespeare's plays and sonnets from a file called "shakespeare.txt". It then tokenizes the text into individual words, stems the words to their base form, and tags the words with their part of speech. Finally, the code can be modified to analyze the text for recurring themes and motifs.

