$$\frac{dN}{dt} = rN\left(1 - \frac{N}{K}\right)$$
where $N$ is the population size, $r$ is the intrinsic growth rate, and $K$ is the carrying capacity of the environment. This equation describes how a population grows exponentially at first, but eventually levels off as it approaches the carrying capacity of the environment.
In the context of a garden, we can interpret $N$ as the number of plants in the garden, $r$ as the growth rate of the plants, and $K$ as the maximum number of plants that can be sustained in the garden given the external factors. We can use this equation to model the growth of the garden over time, taking into account external factors like weather conditions and soil quality that could affect growth.
The significance of these external factors is that they can greatly impact the growth of the garden over time. For example, if the soil quality is poor, the plants may not grow as well and may be more susceptible to disease. Similarly, if the weather conditions are unfavorable, the plants may not receive enough sunlight or water to grow properly. By taking these external factors into account, we can better understand how the garden will develop over time and what steps we can take to ensure its success.

