A common denominator is a number that can be divided by all the denominators of the fractions that we are adding. To find a common denominator, we need to find the least common multiple (LCM) of the denominators.
Here is the Java code to find the common denominator and add the fractions:
```java
import java.util.Scanner;
public class AddFractions {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        System.out.print("Enter the first fraction numerator: ");
        int num1 = sc.nextInt();
        System.out.print("Enter the first fraction denominator: ");
        int den1 = sc.nextInt();
        System.out.print("Enter the second fraction numerator: ");
        int num2 = sc.nextInt();
        System.out.print("Enter the second fraction denominator: ");
        int den2 = sc.nextInt();
        int lcm = findLCM(den1, den2);
        int newNum1 = num1 * (lcm / den1);
        int newNum2 = num2 * (lcm / den2);
        int sumNum = newNum1 + newNum2;
        System.out.println("The sum of " + num1 + "/" + den1 + " and " + num2 + "/" + den2 + " is " + sumNum + "/" + lcm);
    }
    public static int findLCM(int a, int b) {
        int max = Math.max(a, b);
        int min = Math.min(a, b);
        int lcm = max;
        while (lcm % min != 0) {
            lcm += max;
        }
        return lcm;
    }
}
```
In this program, we first ask the user to enter the numerators and denominators of the two fractions. Then, we find the LCM of the denominators using the `findLCM()` method. We use this LCM to convert the fractions to equivalent fractions with the same denominator. Finally, we add the numerators and display the sum as a fraction with the common denominator.

Yes, it is possible to use Swift to create a list of all the art forms showcased at the National Gallery in Washington DC. However, the National Gallery does not provide a public API for accessing their collection data, so you would need to manually scrape the information from their website.
Here's an example code that can help you get started with web scraping using Swift:
```swift
import Foundation
// Define the URL of the National Gallery website
let url = URL(string: "https://www.nga.gov")!
// Create a URLSession to fetch the HTML content
let session = URLSession.shared
let task = session.dataTask(with: url) { (data, response, error) in
    if let error = error {
        print("Error: \(error.localizedDescription)")
        return
    }

    guard let data = data else {
        print("Error: No data received")
        return
    }

    // Convert the HTML data to a string
    let html = String(data: data, encoding: .utf8)!

    // Use regular expressions to extract the art forms and display locations
    let artFormRegex = try! NSRegularExpression(pattern: "<h3 class=\"card__title\">(.*?)</h3>")
    let displayLocationRegex = try! NSRegularExpression(pattern: "<p class=\"card__location\">(.*?)</p>")

    let artFormMatches = artFormRegex.matches(in: html, range: NSRange(html.startIndex..., in: html))
    let displayLocationMatches = displayLocationRegex.matches(in: html, range: NSRange(html.startIndex..., in: html))

    // Print the results
    for i in 0..<artFormMatches.count {
        let artFormRange = artFormMatches[i].range(at: 1)
        let displayLocationRange = displayLocationMatches[i].range(at: 1)
        let artForm = String(html[Range(artFormRange, in: html)!])
        let displayLocation = String(html[Range(displayLocationRange, in: html)!])
        print("\(artForm) - \(displayLocation)")
    }
}
task.resume()
```
This code fetches the HTML content of the National Gallery website and uses regular expressions to extract the art forms and display locations from the relevant HTML tags. Note that this code is just an example and may need to be adapted depending on the specific structure of the National Gallery's website.

