Sure! Here is the PHP code to calculate the total length of the boundary of a regular quadrilateral with sides of length 's' units each using a nested loop:
```
<?php
$s = 5; // length of each side of the quadrilateral
$perimeter = 0; // initialize the perimeter to zero
// loop through each side of the quadrilateral
for ($i = 1; $i <= 4; $i++) {
    // calculate the length of the current side
    $length = $s;
    // if this is not the first side, add the length of the previous side
    if ($i > 1) {
        $length += $prev_side;
    }
    // add the length of the current side to the perimeter
    $perimeter += $length;
    // store the length of the current side for the next iteration
    $prev_side = $length;
}
echo "The perimeter of the regular quadrilateral is: " . $perimeter . " units.";
?>
```
This code uses a `for` loop to iterate through each side of the quadrilateral. Inside the loop, it calculates the length of the current side based on the length of the previous side (if applicable) and adds it to the perimeter. It also stores the length of the current side for the next iteration.
Finally, the code outputs the perimeter of the regular quadrilateral.

