Negotiation is a complex process that involves a variety of factors, including communication skills, emotional intelligence, and the ability to understand and balance the interests of all parties involved. While it is possible to write code that simulates some aspects of negotiation, it is not possible to fully automate the process or provide a comprehensive solution that works in all situations.
That being said, here is an example of how you could implement a basic negotiation strategy in Swift that emphasizes cooperation and compromise:
```swift
// Define the initial offer and counteroffer
var initialOffer = 100
var counterOffer = 50
// Define the minimum and maximum values that are acceptable to both parties
let minAcceptableValue = 60
let maxAcceptableValue = 90
// Loop through the negotiation process until a mutually acceptable value is reached
while true {
    // Determine the midpoint between the two offers
    let midpoint = (initialOffer + counterOffer) / 2

    // If the midpoint is within the acceptable range, accept the offer
    if midpoint >= minAcceptableValue && midpoint <= maxAcceptableValue {
        print("Agreement reached at $\(midpoint)")
        break
    }

    // If the midpoint is higher than the acceptable range, lower the counteroffer
    if midpoint > maxAcceptableValue {
        counterOffer = midpoint
        print("Counteroffer: $\(counterOffer)")
    }

    // If the midpoint is lower than the acceptable range, raise the initial offer
    if midpoint < minAcceptableValue {
        initialOffer = midpoint
        print("Initial offer: $\(initialOffer)")
    }
}
```
In this example, we start with an initial offer of $100 and a counteroffer of $50. We then define the minimum and maximum values that are acceptable to both parties as $60 and $90, respectively.
We then enter a loop that calculates the midpoint between the two offers and checks whether it falls within the acceptable range. If it does, we print a message indicating that an agreement has been reached and break out of the loop.
If the midpoint is higher than the acceptable range, we lower the counteroffer to the midpoint and print a message indicating the new counteroffer. If the midpoint is lower than the acceptable range, we raise the initial offer to the midpoint and print a message indicating the new initial offer.
This process continues until a mutually acceptable value is reached, at which point the loop terminates and we print a message indicating the final agreed-upon value.
Of course, this is a very simplified example and does not take into account many of the complexities and nuances of real-world negotiation. However, it provides a basic framework that can be adapted and expanded upon to suit specific negotiation scenarios.

