```
function generateDataMap(sentence) {
  const dataMap = {
    intendedPurchase: '',
    actualPurchase: [],
    quantity: {}
  };
  const words = sentence.split(' ');
  for (let i = 0; i < words.length; i++) {
    const word = words[i];
    if (word === 'intending' || word === 'intended') {
      dataMap.intendedPurchase = words[i + 2];
    }
    if (word === 'to' && words[i + 1] === 'buy') {
      const item = words[i + 2];
      if (item !== 'oranges') {
        dataMap.actualPurchase.push(item);
      } else {
        const quantity = words[i - 1];
        if (quantity in dataMap.quantity) {
          dataMap.quantity[quantity]++;
        } else {
          dataMap.quantity[quantity] = 1;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return dataMap;
}
```
This updated function now includes a `quantity` property in the `dataMap` object, which is an object that stores the quantity of oranges that the person intended to purchase. The function first checks if the sentence contains the word "intending" or "intended" to determine if the person had intended to purchase oranges. If so, it stores the quantity of oranges in the `quantity` property. If the sentence contains other items that the person actually purchased, those items are stored in the `actualPurchase` array. 
This function can handle multiple sentences with similar structures by simply passing each sentence through the `generateDataMap` function one at a time.

