The possible number of skill combinations that can be formed from a group of 20 people if a team of 5 people needs to be created is:
Formula: 20! / (5! * (20-5)!) = 15,504
Java code:
```
public static int calculateCombinations(int n, int r) {
    int numerator = 1;
    int denominator = 1;
    for (int i = n; i >= n - r + 1; i--) {
        numerator *= i;
    }
    for (int i = r; i >= 1; i--) {
        denominator *= i;
    }
    return numerator / denominator;
}
int numCombinations = calculateCombinations(20, 5);
System.out.println("Number of possible team combinations: " + numCombinations);
```
If the team is required to have at least 2 people with a specific skill set, we can calculate the number of possible team combinations that meet this requirement by subtracting the number of combinations where none of the specific skill set members are present from the total number of combinations:
Formula: (number of combinations without specific skill set members) - (number of combinations without any restrictions)
Java code:
```
int numCombinationsWithoutRestrictions = calculateCombinations(20, 5);
int numCombinationsWithoutSpecificSkillSet = calculateCombinations(18, 5);
int numCombinationsWithSpecificSkillSet = numCombinationsWithoutSpecificSkillSet - numCombinationsWithoutRestrictions;
System.out.println("Number of possible team combinations with specific skill set: " + numCombinationsWithSpecificSkillSet);
```
In this case, we subtract the number of combinations where we pick 5 people from the remaining 18 people without any restrictions, from the total number of combinations where we pick 5 people from the entire group of 20 people. This gives us the number of possible team combinations that meet the requirement of having at least 2 people with a specific skill set.

